    id: 'fft-resolution', title: 'FFT, resolution, peaks, harmonics and drift', summary: 'A plain-language tour of the measurements on screen and what they do not prove.',
    text: `A discrete Fourier transform expresses a finite sample window as contributions at a set of frequencies. The fast Fourier transform, or FFT, is an efficient way to calculate that transform. ZionCore uses a selected FFT size and the input sample rate to space frequency bins. The basic spacing is sample rate divided by FFT size. For example, at 48,000 samples per second with 4,096 points, adjacent bins are 11.71875 Hz apart. A larger transform gives closer-spaced bins, but it also requires a longer sample window and does not magically improve a microphone's calibration or distinguish two sources that overlap in time.

The tallest peak is the strongest bin under the current analysis conditions, not automatically the most important sound in the room. A real tone may spread energy into nearby bins because its frequency sits between bins, because the window is short, or because it changes while sampled. Windowing reduces some forms of spectral leakage while changing the appearance of amplitude. A peak finder can report a convenient frequency estimate, but its precision should not be confused with the physical accuracy of the device. Treat displayed decimals as a representation, not a promise of laboratory-grade certainty.

Harmonics are components near integer multiples of a lower fundamental. A 120 Hz tone, for example, may have energy near 240 Hz and 360 Hz; the pattern can indicate a periodic source, but several instruments, motors, room resonances, and overlapping sounds can produce related structures. The strongest component may be a harmonic rather than the fundamental. A harmonic relationship is useful evidence about periodicity, not a unique fingerprint of a machine, animal, person, or spiritual source.

Drift describes how a measured component appears to move over time. Small changes can result from a changing source, temperature, rotation speed, movement, microphone position, automatic gain, or analysis noise. Stability values summarize how consistent the estimated dominant frequency was in the observed windows. Amplitude variation describes changes in level, not a diagnosis of intent. When a pattern moves, note its direction, time span, and capture conditions; avoid turning “changed” into “warned” or “safe.”

RMS level is a summary of signal magnitude over a window. It is not a calibrated sound-pressure level unless the whole recording chain has been calibrated. Relative level changes can still help compare sessions if the hardware, placement, gain, and conditions are kept consistent. Spectral flatness and entropy describe aspects of how energy is distributed; neither measures emotional quality or spiritual character. Any single feature is a narrow view. Reading several together, and noting what each cannot answer, produces a more responsible account.`
